Z||000000||PatrickB||12-17-2000||11:30 PM||||My Fiero doesnt start now.  My mom went to move it from my driveway and coincidence or not it doesnt start.  She said she tried putting it in gear, the car jumped forward and she gave up. <BR>When I went out there the car barely ran and sounded like it was running on two cyl.  Now it does not start or run at all.  <BR>So far the only thing I have checked for is spark which is perfect. (Its been raining since).  The only other thing I can think of is a fuel problem or maybe the timing is off?<P>Any suggestions would be greatfully appreciated!<P>Pat||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||12-17-2000||11:38 PM||||Are we suposed to guess what year and motor it is?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-18-2000||12:07 AM||||Bad distributor cap? Water in distributor?<BR>Bad ignition module? Bad injector(s)?<BR>Could be many things. Guessing it's a 4-cyl since you mention "running on 2 cylinders" If it's an 87/88 with DIS could be bad coil pack. Pre-87 could be weak coil. Spark plug wires could be bad. Any more info would be helpful. Like, How was the car running when you parked it? Was it raining then? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PatrickB||12-18-2000||11:31 AM||||Sorry, I forgot to put the year.  Its an 86 GT. <P>The coil and ignition module are brand new and I checked the spark which is fine. <BR>The car was running fine when I parked it the night before.  As soon as I started it it started running like junk.<p>[This message has been edited by PatrickB (edited 12-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-18-2000||08:20 PM||||check the 2 injector fuses.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AL||12-18-2000||09:49 PM||||check the fuel pump fuse while your there also.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||12-19-2000||03:29 PM||||Two more possibilities come to mind (<B>after</B> you try the fuses):<P>1) Cold start injector switch.  If you think it's flooding when you crank it, try unplugging the cold start switch and see what happens.  Disclaimer: Never had this problem, myself, but it's worth a try.<P>2) Timing chain slipped - especially if car had been sitting in cold weather for awhile, before the no-start.  Mark distributor position (so you can return it to where you found it).  Then advance the ignition timing (turn distributor CCW) while someone else cranks the engine, and see if you can get it to fire.  If so, you'll probably need to replace the timing chain.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
